    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Swahili grew from the Bantu speech of the East African coast, where from around the 8th century merchant towns traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, absorbing many Arabic words.</li><li>Written in Arabic script from at least the early 18th century, it developed a rich poetic tradition, and in the 19th century caravans carried it inland from Zanzibar as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> and the Congo.</li><li>Spread by colonial administrations and, after independence, promoted by Tanzania as its national language, it is today the common language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and one of the most widely spoken languages of the continent.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>, the first president of Tanzania, translated Shakespeare’s Julius Caesar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-merchant-of-venice">The Merchant of Venice</a> into Swahili, to show that the language could carry any literature in the world.</p></aside>
    </section>

